section.para:
Redis authentication and database selection:

upstream redis_backend {
    server    127.0.0.1:6379;
    keepalive 4;

    db_type redis;
}

server {
    ...
    location = /lookup {
            db_pass redis_backend;
            db_name 3;
            db_auth "secret";
            db_get  $arg_key;
    }
}

Per-database lookup caching:

keyval_zone zone=dbcache_db0:1m timeout=60s;
keyval_zone zone=dbcache_db1:1m timeout=60s;

upstream redis_backend {
    server    127.0.0.1:6379;
    keepalive 4;

    db_type redis;
}

server {
    ...
    location /db0 {
        db_pass  redis_backend;
        db_name  0;
        db_get   $arg_key;
        db_cache dbcache_db0;
    }

    location /db1 {
        db_pass  redis_backend;
        db_name  1;
        db_get   $arg_key;
        db_cache dbcache_db1;
    }
}

Verified TLS and mutual TLS connection:

location /db {
    db_pass redis_backend;
    db_get  $arg_key;

    db_ssl                     on;
    db_ssl_verify              on;
    db_ssl_trusted_certificate ca.crt;
    db_ssl_name                localhost;

    db_ssl_certificate         client.crt;
    db_ssl_certificate_key     client.key;
}

Using database-backed variables, for example,
retrieving a resource owner from Redis and adding it to a response header.

location = /internal/db-get {
    internal;
    db_pass redis_backend;
    db_get  $arg_key;
}

location /resource {
    db_var     $owner /internal/db-get "key=$arg_id" default=unknown;
    add_header X-Owner $owner;
    proxy_pass http://application;
}

directive.para:
Specifies the password string
used to authenticate connections to a database server.
An empty value disables authentication.
Authentication with a separate username and password arguments
is not supported.

location /db/ {
    db_pass redis_backend;
    db_auth secret;
    db_get  $arg_key;
}

The transparent parameter allows outgoing connections to a database server originate from a non-local IP address, for example, from a real IP address of a client:
db_bind $remote_addr transparent;
In order for this parameter to work, it is usually necessary to run nginx worker processes with the superuser privileges. On Linux it is not required as if the transparent parameter is specified, worker processes inherit the CAP_NET_RAW capability from the master process. It is also necessary to configure kernel routing table to intercept network traffic from the database server.

For example:

http {
    keyval_zone zone=db_cache:10m timeout=1m;

    upstream redis_backend {
        db_type redis;
        server  127.0.0.1:6379;
    }

    server {
        location /db/ {
            db_pass redis_backend;
            db_get  $arg_key;

            db_cache db_cache timeout=30s max_size=128k not_found=5s;
        }
    }
}

For the Redis backend, the key is passed to the GET command.
A successful lookup returns the value as the response body.

upstream redis_backend {
    db_type redis;
    server  127.0.0.1:6379;
}

server {
    location /db/ {
        db_pass redis_backend;
        db_get  "user:$arg_id";
    }
}

For the Redis backend, the parameter specifies the database index
passed to the SELECT command. 
If the directive is not specified or its value is empty,
database 0 is used:

location /db/ {
    db_pass redis_backend;
    db_name 3;
    db_get  $arg_key;
}

Specifies the conditions under which a request can be passed to the next database server:
error an error occurred while establishing a connection with the server, passing a request to it, or reading the response header;
timeout a timeout has occurred while establishing a connection with the server, passing a request to it, or reading the response header;
invalid_response
server returned an invalid response. For the Redis backend, this also includes Redis error replies, such as authentication failures or key-value mismatch errors;
not_found
the requested key was not found. For the Redis backend, this corresponds to a nil reply to the GET command;
The parameter cannot be used when caching of not_found results is enabled with the directive.
off
disables passing a request to the next server.

Specifies the upstream group used for database lookups:
db_pass redis_backend;
The group must be configured with . A server address cannot be specified directly.

The directive has the following parameters:



max


sets the maximum number of elements in the cache;
on cache overflow the least recently used (LRU) elements are removed;



inactive


defines a time after which an element is removed from the cache
if it has not been accessed during this time;
by default, it is 10 seconds;



valid


defines a time during which
an element in the cache is considered valid
and can be reused;
by default, it is 60 seconds.
Certificates that exceed this time will be reloaded or revalidated;



off


disables the cache.

Sets the database protocol used by the upstream group.
Currently, only redis is supported:

upstream redis_backend {
    server  127.0.0.1:6379;
    db_type redis;
}

The protocol name is case-insensitive.

Creates a variable whose value is obtained from the response body of a subrequest. The location parameter sets the subrequest URI. The query parameter sets its query string. An empty query string can be specified explicitly:
db_var $configuration /_configuration "";
The parameter values can contain text, variables, and their combination.
